From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:49:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ionic: put headers in the linear area on header split queues

Teach the Rx path what to do on a queue that was created with
IONIC_RXQ_F_HDS. Nothing creates one yet; the next patch does.

On such a queue the descriptor's own buffer is a small header buffer,
IONIC_HDS_HDR_BUF_LEN (256) bytes from a second page pool that the
queue gets for the purpose, and the SG elements are whole pages from
the queue's existing pool. The device says in the completion how many
bytes it wrote to the header buffer, and whether that is exactly the
headers. The skb is built from that alone: those bytes are copied to
the linear area of a napi_alloc_skb() skb, the header buffer goes back
to its pool, and the SG buffers the packet used become the frags. The
packet is not parsed to find the split.

The payload is never touched. There is no copybreak on a header split
queue (the header is copied anyway, and the payload must not be), XDP
is refused with header split in the next patch, and the checksum comes
from the device. This is what makes it safe to let the payload pool
hand out memory the kernel can't read, in the last patch.

Two kinds of packet can't be handed to the stack if their frags are
unreadable, and are dropped and counted instead:

 - one the device did not split but that is longer than the header
   buffer. It has more than TCP payload in its SG buffers, and the
   stack would have to pull headers out of them.

 - one that would need a software checksum, because Rx checksum offload
   was turned off or the device did not vouch for the packet.

With readable buffers both are received normally.

The device's numbers are checked against what the descriptor was given
before any buffer is taken from it, so that a packet that is dropped
leaves its buffers in place to be posted again.

The SG buffers are sized for a whole packet, not a packet less the
header buffer: the device may split after 54 bytes or not at all. They
are whole pages so that a payload never shares a page with another
packet's.

A side effect is that a header split queue has room for a frame of up
to MTU + 18 + 256 bytes where another queue has room for MTU + 18: a
device that does not split a frame fills the header buffer and then the
SG buffers. Such a frame is received, not dropped.

